  ]}
>
  <h2>Protein: how much and how often</h2>
  <p>Jordan's rule of thumb is a gram of protein per pound of body weight. The research range is lower, and the two are worth comparing for a {bwLb} lb person:</p>
  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <table class="pct-table data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Daily protein for {bwLb} lb</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="wrap">ISSN position stand: 1.4 to 2.0 g per kg for most exercising people (Jager et al., 2017)</td><td>{issnLow} to {issnHigh} g</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="wrap">Meta-analysis of 49 trials: no extra muscle gain beyond about 1.6 g per kg (Morton et al., 2018)</td><td>about {morton} g</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="wrap">Jordan's rule: 1 g per lb</td><td>{jordan} g</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Jordan's rule is easy to remember and sits above the point where the studies stopped finding added gains, so if you are already near it, more protein is unlikely to be the problem. The <a href="/nutrition-calculators/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> works out your own range.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Timing.</strong> Jordan says the post-workout window does not matter. The ISSN says the best timing is likely individual, since benefits come from protein before or after training, and the effect of a workout lasts at least 24 hours. It suggests spreading protein across the day, roughly every 3 to 4 hours, with about 0.25 g per kg or 20 to 40 g per serving. Total daily protein matters most.</li>
    <li><strong>Quality.</strong> Jordan's one specific food advice is to get complete proteins. All animal-derived proteins are complete, with all the essential and non-essential amino acids.</li>
    <li><strong>Supplements.</strong> Whey is another complete source and an easy way to get protein in, but Jordan says you should not rely on shakes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Calories</h2>
  <p>To gain muscle, Jordan estimates maintenance calories and adds 300 to 500. If you are eating at or below maintenance and your bench has stalled, check this before anything else. The <a href="/nutrition-calculators/tdee-calculator/">TDEE calculator</a> estimates maintenance for you. Jordan's tip for busy people is to have food at home that you know you will eat that day, since being unprepared and hungry at the end of the day leads to grabbing the closest food.</p>

  <h2>Sleep</h2>
  <p>Jordan calls sleep critically important for everything to do with fitness, and says to get as much as you possibly can. His tips for bad sleepers:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Get into a routine.</li>
    <li>Keep the room cool and crisp.</li>
    <li>Take a hot shower at night, so your body cools afterward.</li>
    <li>Avoid bright lights and screens before bed.</li>
    <li>Eat enough calories and protein through the day so hunger does not wake you.</li>
    <li>Wind down stress at the end of the day, for example by reading.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>This is Jordan's advice from his coaching interview. We did not cite a study for it.</p>

  <h2>Creatine</h2>
  <p>The ISSN position stand on creatine (Kreider and colleagues, 2017) reports that creatine supplementation raises muscle creatine and improves high-intensity exercise performance, leading to greater training adaptations. It also concludes that short- and long-term supplementation, in studies of up to 30 grams a day for 5 years, is safe and well tolerated in healthy people.</p>
  <p>Jordan's coaching answers on how to use it, which are his and not from the paper:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Handle the basics first, and get health advice from your doctor.</li>
    <li>He starts people around 5 grams a day and raises it as needed. An alternative he describes is a loading phase of 10 to 20 grams for the first 10 days, then a lower maintenance dose. The paper's abstract does not give these doses.</li>
    <li>Take it at a time you will remember every day. Consistency matters. Many people take it in the morning or after training.</li>
    <li>It can raise your weight because muscle holds more water, which is not fat gain.</li>
    <li>Women may not need as much, because women tend to be smaller overall.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>He also says that if caffeine or a pre-workout affects your sleep, it is a poor trade for the workout.</p>

  <h2>If all of that is fine</h2>
  <p>Then the cause is probably in the training. Jordan's first suspects for a stall are technique, then recovery and hidden injuries, then programming. See <a href="/why-my-bench-press-is-weak/">why your bench is weak</a> and <a href="/bench-press-programming-for-plateaus/">programming for plateaus</a>.</p>
</ArticlePage>
